Writing your own functional interface.

The java-util-function shapes cover most needs, so most of the time you shouldn't write your own. But sometimes a lambda deserves a named, domain-specific type — for readability, for an extra type parameter the built-ins don't offer, or to declare a checked exception. Knowing how, and when, rounds out the picture.

The recipe is simple. A functional interface is just an interface with exactly one abstract method — a SAM. And you mark it at-Functional-Interface, so the compiler enforces that. It's a safety net: the build fails if someone later adds a second abstract method. So a discount-rule interface, marked at-Functional-Interface, declares a single abstract method — apply, taking an order, returning a big-decimal. Now a lambda can implement it: discount-rule loyalty, equals, order, arrow, the order's total times nine-tenths. And you call it with loyalty dot apply of an order. The lovely thing is that discount-rule reads better than function of order to big-decimal — the name says what the function is, not just its shape.

Now, an important allowance: default and static methods are fine. The "single abstract method" rule counts only abstract methods, so a functional interface may add as many default and static methods as it likes. That's exactly how function itself carries and-then and compose, and how predicate carries and, or, and negate. So you can give your own interface composition helpers too. Discount-rule could add a default method, and-cap, taking a max, that returns order, arrow, apply the rule and then take the minimum with max. It still has just one abstract method. And Object's methods, like equals, also don't count against the SAM limit.

So when should you actually write one? The honest guidance is: prefer a built-in, unless a specific reason pushes you off it. First, domain clarity — a named type like discount-rule, retry-policy, or validator-of-T reads far better than a bare function or predicate, in an API that others will call. That's worth it for a public, central abstraction. Second, a shape the built-ins lack — for example, three inputs, since there's no tri-function, or an unusual mix of arity and primitives. And third, checked exceptions — the standard interfaces' methods declare no checked exceptions, so a lambda that throws one, say an I-O exception, simply won't fit them. A custom functional interface, whose method declares "throws I-O exception", solves it — like an I-O-supplier of T, whose get throws I-O exception.

And when should you not? If your interface would just re-say function of T-R, or predicate of T, with no added meaning, use the built-in. A proliferation of near-identical one-method interfaces is friction, not clarity, and callers already know the standard ones. So the rule of thumb: reach for a standard functional interface first, and define your own only when a name, an arity, or a checked exception genuinely earns it. With the interfaces covered, the next section opens up how a lambda relates to the code around it — the variables it captures.
